**Sec. 80001.** This section amends the Higher Education Act to exempt specific family assets from being counted in the calculation of a student's financial aid eligibility. The exempted assets include a family farm on which the family resides, a small business with no more than 100 full-time or full-time equivalent employees owned and controlled by the family, and a commercial fishing business including vessels and permits. These exemptions take effect on July 1, 2026, and apply to award year 2026-2027 and each subsequent award year.

**Sec. 81001.** This section establishes new loan limits for graduate and professional students and parent borrowers, effective July 1, 2026, while terminating the authority to make new Federal Direct PLUS loans to graduate and professional students. Graduate students may borrow up to $20,500 annually with a $100,000 aggregate limit, while professional students may borrow up to $50,000 annually with a $200,000 aggregate limit. Parent borrowers of dependent students are capped at $20,000 annually and $65,000 in aggregate for Federal Direct PLUS loans. A lifetime maximum aggregate loan limit of $257,500 is established for all students, excluding parent PLUS loans, with interim exceptions for students already enrolled as of June 30, 2026.

**Sec. 82001.** This section transitions borrowers from income contingent repayment plans to new income-based plans by July 1, 2028, and establishes a new "Repayment Assistance Plan" (RAP) effective July 1, 2026. The RAP sets monthly payments based on a tiered percentage of adjusted gross income, ranging from a flat $120 for incomes under $10,000 to 10% of income for those over $100,000, minus $50 per dependent. Borrowers who make 360 qualifying monthly payments under the RAP will have their remaining loan balance canceled. The section also repeals the authority for income contingent repayment plans and restricts new loans made on or after July 1, 2026, to either a standard repayment plan or the RAP.

**Sec. 82002.** This section sunsets eligibility for unemployment and economic hardship deferments for borrowers who receive loans on or after July 1, 2027. Additionally, it limits forbearance for loans made on or after July 1, 2027, to a maximum of 9 months during any 24-month period. These changes aim to reduce the duration of non-payment periods for new student loans.

**Sec. 82003.** This section allows borrowers to rehabilitate defaulted Federal Family Education Loan (FFEL) and Perkins loans up to two times, rather than the previous limit of one time. It also sets a minimum total monthly payment of $10 for borrowers with one or more loans made under part D on or after July 1, 2027, who are in default. These amendments take effect beginning on July 1, 2027.

**Sec. 82004.** This section amends the Public Service Loan Forgiveness program to count on-time payments made under the new Repayment Assistance Plan toward the required 120 qualifying payments. This ensures that borrowers using the new income-based plan can still achieve loan forgiveness after working in qualifying public service jobs.

**Sec. 82005.** This section appropriates $1,000,000,000 to the Secretary of Education for administrative costs, including the servicing of direct student loan programs. These funds are available in addition to other appropriated amounts and remain available until expended.

**Sec. 83001.** This section amends the definition of adjusted gross income for Federal Pell Grant eligibility to include foreign income for academic years beginning on or after July 1, 2026. It also establishes ineligibility for the Federal Pell Grant for students whose Student Aid Index equals or exceeds twice the total maximum Federal Pell Grant amount for that academic year. These changes take effect on July 1, 2026.

**Sec. 83002.** This section creates a new "Workforce Pell Grant" program effective for award year 2026-2027, providing grants to students enrolled in eligible short-term workforce programs. Eligible programs must be between 150 and 600 clock hours in length, last between 8 and 15 weeks, and meet specific state and federal criteria regarding completion rates, job placement, and earnings outcomes. Students cannot concurrently receive a standard Federal Pell Grant and a Workforce Pell Grant.

**Sec. 83003.** This section increases the Pell Grant shortfall threshold from $2,170,000,000 to $12,670,000,000. This change affects the calculation of the maximum Pell Grant award when the total cost of attendance exceeds the maximum grant amount.

**Sec. 83004.** This section makes students ineligible for the Federal Pell Grant during any period in which they receive non-Federal grant aid (from states, institutions, or private sources) that equals or exceeds their cost of attendance. This exclusion takes effect on July 1, 2026.

**Sec. 84001.** This section establishes ineligibility for federal funds for educational programs at institutions of higher education if the median earnings of program graduates are less than the median earnings of comparable working adults for two of the three years preceding the determination. The comparison is based on working adults aged 25-34 with similar education levels, using data from the Bureau of the Census. Institutions must notify students if a program is at risk of losing eligibility and may appeal the determination.

**Sec. 85001.** This section delays the implementation of new borrower defense to repayment regulations published in November 2022 for loans originating before July 1, 2035. It restores the borrower defense regulations that were in effect on July 1, 2020, for those loans.

**Sec. 85002.** This section delays the implementation of new closed school discharge regulations published in November 2022 for loans originating before July 1, 2035. It reverts the relevant Code of Federal Regulations provisions to their status prior to the 2022 amendments for those loans.

**Sec. 86001.** This section appropriates $40,000,000 to the National Endowment for the Humanities for fiscal year 2025, available through fiscal year 2028, for the procurement of statues. The funds are intended to support projects described in Executive Orders 13934, 13978, and 14189, relating to monuments and the National Garden of American Heroes.

**Sec. 87001.** This section appropriates $300,000,000 to the Office of Refugee Resettlement for fiscal year 2025, available until September 30, 2028, for vetting potential sponsors of unaccompanied alien children. Funds may be used for background checks, home studies, gang-related tattoo examinations, data system improvements, and coordination with state child welfare agencies. [end of text]


